// The edit that looks like it goes both ways, and does not. Paired with
// edit_diverges_both_ways_old.dw, which is the version it replaces.
//
// This file exists for ONE assertion: that a permissiveness verdict is read from a session's
// FIRST divergence and nothing after it. Every other fixture gives the same answer with or
// without that rule, so without this pair the `~Diverged` guard in Vacuity.tla is a soundness
// argument nothing checks.
//
// The session that discriminates:
//
//   attempt 1   Trade     this file PERMITS, the old file denies      -> the edit WIDENED
//                         and the attempt records a Trade::response
//   attempt 2   Approve   this file FORBIDS -- a Trade just happened  -> looks like NARROWING
//                         -- the old file permits
//
// The second observation is not evidence about the old file. It sits on a history that only THIS
// file can produce: under the old file attempt 1 was denied, so no Trade::response was ever
// recorded, so the forbid below never arms and the divergence never arises. Counting it would
// report INCOMPARABLE -- "this edit both adds and removes permissions" -- on the strength of a
// trajectory the old policy cannot reach.
//
// The honest verdict is MORE PERMISSIVE, and that is what the guard produces.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Approve",
    resource
);

// Armed only by a completed Trade, which is the whole point: under the old policy no Trade ever
// completes, so this rule is unreachable there.
forbid (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Approve",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1000s Anchor::Action::"Trade"::response{ }
};
